window.__labLoaded({"id":"7.2","title":"ANOVA: Comparing Three or More Means","goal":"Compare the exam scores of three teaching methods with a one-way ANOVA, then use Tukey's HSD to find which pairs of methods differ.","question":"An instructor taught three groups of five students with three methods (lecture, flipped classroom, problem-based), then gave all 15 the same exam. The group means are 80.0, 88.8 and 71.0. Do the teaching methods really differ, or is this the ordinary scatter you get between any 15 students? If they differ, which methods differ from which?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the three groups","text":"Five students per teaching method, the same 15 scores as the lesson. We look at each group mean first, because ANOVA asks whether those means are further apart than the spread inside each group would explain.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multicomp import pairwise_tukeyhsd\n\nlecture = [78, 82, 85, 79, 76]\nflipped = [88, 91, 87, 93, 85]\nproblem = [72, 68, 74, 70, 71]\n\nfor name, g in [('lecture', lecture), ('flipped', flipped), ('problem', problem)]:\n    print(f'{name:8s} mean = {np.mean(g):.1f}   sd = {np.std(g, ddof=1):.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for the numbers, here to get means and standard deviations.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which include the one-way ANOVA.","Load Tukey's HSD from statsmodels, the follow-up test that compares every pair of groups.","Exam scores of the five students taught by lecture.","Scores of the five students in the flipped classroom.","Scores of the five students in the problem-based class.","Loop over (label, scores) pairs so we write the summary once for all three groups.","Print each group's mean and its sample SD (ddof=1 divides by n - 1). ANOVA asks whether the means are further apart than this spread allows."],"meaning":"Lecture averages 80.0, flipped 88.8 and problem-based 71.0. The SDs inside each group are small (2.24 to 3.54) compared with the 8 to 18 point gaps between the means, which hints that the methods differ.","out":"lecture  mean = 80.0   sd = 3.54\nflipped  mean = 88.8   sd = 3.19\nproblem  mean = 71.0   sd = 2.24","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the one-way ANOVA","text":"f_oneway compares the spread between group means with the spread inside the groups. F near 1 means no difference; a large F with a small p-value says at least one mean differs.","code":"res = stats.f_oneway(lecture, flipped, problem)\n\nprint(f'F       = {res.statistic:.2f}')\nprint('df      = (2, 12)')\nprint(f'p-value = {res.pvalue:.7f}')","explain":["f_oneway runs the one-way ANOVA: it compares the spread between the group means with the spread inside the groups.","F is the ratio of those two spreads. Near 1 means no difference; larger means the means are further apart than chance explains.","Degrees of freedom: 3 groups - 1 = 2 between groups, and 15 - 3 = 12 within groups.","The p-value is how rare an F this large would be if all three methods really had the same mean."],"meaning":"F = 42.90 with 2 and 12 df gives p = 0.0000034. If the three methods truly had the same mean, an F this large would almost never happen, so at least one method differs from the others. It does not yet say which one.","out":"F       = 42.90\ndf      = (2, 12)\np-value = 0.0000034","figs":[]},{"title":"Measure the effect size","text":"Eta squared is the share of all the score variation that is explained by teaching method. It is the between-group sum of squares divided by the total.","code":"scores = np.array(lecture + flipped + problem)\ngrand = scores.mean()\n\nssb = sum(len(g) * (np.mean(g) - grand) ** 2 for g in (lecture, flipped, problem))\nsst = ((scores - grand) ** 2).sum()\n\nprint(f'between SS = {ssb:.1f}, total SS = {sst:.1f}')\nprint(f'eta squared = {ssb / sst:.3f}')","explain":["Put all 15 scores in one array so we can find the overall mean and total variation.","The grand mean is the average of all 15 scores. Every sum of squares is measured from it.","Between-group sum of squares: each group's mean distance from the grand mean, squared, times its size.","Total sum of squares: how far all 15 scores are from the grand mean, squared and added up.","Print both sums of squares so we can see how much of the total is between groups.","Eta squared = between / total, the share of all score variation explained by teaching method."],"meaning":"Between-group SS is 792.1 out of a total of 902.9, so eta squared = 0.877. About 88% of the variation in scores goes with teaching method, a very large effect. The rest is ordinary student-to-student scatter.","out":"between SS = 792.1, total SS = 902.9\neta squared = 0.877","figs":[]},{"title":"Find which pairs differ with Tukey","text":"A significant ANOVA only says some mean is different. Tukey's HSD compares every pair while keeping the overall false-alarm rate at 5%. In the table, reject = True means that pair differs.","code":"labels = ['lecture'] * 5 + ['flipped'] * 5 + ['problem'] * 5\n\ntukey = pairwise_tukeyhsd(scores, labels, alpha=0.05)\n\nprint(tukey.summary())","explain":["Make one label per score ('lecture' five times, and so on) in the same order as the scores.","Run Tukey's HSD at alpha 0.05. It compares every pair of groups while holding the overall false-alarm rate at 5%.","Print the table. meandiff is group2 minus group1, and reject = True means that pair really differs."],"meaning":"Every pair differs. Lecture scored 8.8 below flipped (p = 0.0017), problem-based 17.8 below flipped (p < 0.0001) and 9.0 below lecture (p = 0.0014). So flipped is best, lecture is in the middle, and problem-based is lowest on this exam.","out":"  Multiple Comparison of Means - Tukey HSD, FWER=0.05   \n========================================================\n group1  group2 meandiff p-adj   lower    upper   reject\n--------------------------------------------------------\nflipped lecture     -8.8 0.0017 -13.9271  -3.6729   True\nflipped problem    -17.8    0.0 -22.9271 -12.6729   True\nlecture problem     -9.0 0.0014 -14.1271  -3.8729   True\n--------------------------------------------------------","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"score <- c(78, 82, 85, 79, 76, 88, 91, 87, 93, 85, 72, 68, 74, 70, 71)\nmethod <- factor(rep(c(\"lecture\", \"flipped\", \"problem\"), each = 5))\nfit <- aov(score ~ method)\nsummary(fit)\nTukeyHSD(fit)","expect":"method: Df 2, Sum Sq 792.1, Mean Sq 396.1, F value 42.9, Pr(>F) 3.41e-06; residuals Df 12, Sum Sq 110.8, Mean Sq 9.2; Tukey differences: lecture-flipped -8.8, problem-flipped -17.8, problem-lecture -9.0 (adjusted p 0.0017, 0.0000, 0.0014)","note":"R and the Python table both order the groups alphabetically (flipped, lecture, problem), so the differences match. summary(fit) prints the p-value as 3.41e-06, which means 0.00000341.","explain":["Enter the 15 scores in order: five lecture, then five flipped, then five problem-based.","Make a grouping variable (factor) that repeats each method name five times, in the same order as the scores.","aov fits the one-way ANOVA. The formula score ~ method reads 'score explained by method'.","summary prints the ANOVA table with the F value and its p-value.","TukeyHSD compares every pair of methods and adjusts the p-values so the overall false-alarm rate stays at 5%."],"read":"In the ANOVA table, read F value 42.9 and Pr(>F) 3.41e-06 (that is 0.00000341): the methods differ. In the Tukey table, diff is one group minus the other and p adj below 0.05 means that pair differs: all three pairs do (-8.8, -17.8, -9.0)."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > One-Way ANOVA (Post Hoc: Tukey)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / method score.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 78  1 82  1 85  1 79  1 76\n2 88  2 91  2 87  2 93  2 85\n3 72  3 68  3 74  3 70  3 71\nEND DATA.\nONEWAY score BY method\n  /POSTHOC=TUKEY ALPHA(0.05).","note":"Method codes: 1 lecture, 2 flipped, 3 problem-based. The ANOVA table gives F and Sig.; the Multiple Comparisons table gives each Tukey pair.","explain":["Declare two variables, the teaching method code and the exam score, to be read in free format.","Start of the inline data.","Lecture students (method 1): each pair is the method code then the score.","Flipped classroom students (method 2).","Problem-based students (method 3).","End of the inline data.","ONEWAY runs a one-way ANOVA of score across the groups of method.","Add Tukey's post hoc comparisons at the 0.05 level. The full stop ends the command."],"read":"In the ANOVA table read F = 42.900 and Sig. = 0.000: the methods differ. In Multiple Comparisons (Tukey HSD) read Mean Difference and Sig. for each pair. All three Sig. values are below 0.05, so every pair differs. 1 = lecture, 2 = flipped, 3 = problem-based."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 15\ngenerate score = real(word(\"78 82 85 79 76 88 91 87 93 85 72 68 74 70 71\", _n))\ngenerate method = ceil(_n / 5)\noneway score method\nquietly anova score method\npwcompare method, mcompare(tukey) effects","note":"oneway prints the ANOVA table with F and Prob > F. pwcompare after anova gives every pairwise difference with Tukey-adjusted p-values. Methods: 1 lecture, 2 flipped, 3 problem-based.","explain":["Clear any data already in memory.","Create 15 empty rows, one per student.","Fill the score column. word() picks the n-th number from the list and real() turns it into a number, a quick way to type the data.","Make the method code: ceil(_n / 5) gives 1 for rows 1 to 5, 2 for rows 6 to 10 and 3 for rows 11 to 15.","oneway prints the ANOVA table of score by method with F and Prob > F.","Fit the same model with anova, quietly so nothing is printed, because pwcompare needs it.","pwcompare compares every pair of methods; mcompare(tukey) gives Tukey-adjusted p-values and effects shows the differences."],"read":"In the oneway table find F = 42.90 and Prob > F = 0.0000: the methods differ. In the pwcompare table, the Contrast column is the difference in means (for example 2 vs 1 is 8.8) and the Tukey p-value column shows each pair is below 0.05."},"conclusion":"Yes, the teaching methods differ. The flipped classroom averaged 88.8, lecture 80.0 and problem-based 71.0, and every pair differs (ANOVA F = 42.90, p < 0.0001; eta squared = 0.88). Caution: with only five students per method, and no random assignment mentioned, this does not prove that the method itself caused the differences.","practice":{"scenario":"A gardener grows tomato plants with three fertilisers (A, B and C), six plants each, and records the total fruit weight per plant in kilograms. Do the fertilisers produce different yields, and if so, which ones differ?","data":"a = [21, 24, 19, 23, 22, 20]\nb = [27, 30, 26, 29, 31, 28]\nc = [22, 25, 24, 21, 26, 23]","tasks":["Find the mean yield for each fertiliser.","Run a one-way ANOVA and report F and the p-value.","Compute eta squared.","Use Tukey's HSD to say which pairs of fertilisers differ."],"hint":"Use stats.f_oneway(a, b, c), then pairwise_tukeyhsd on all 18 values with a matching list of labels. Eta squared is between SS divided by total SS.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom statsmodels.stats.multicomp import pairwise_tukeyhsd\n\na = [21, 24, 19, 23, 22, 20]\nb = [27, 30, 26, 29, 31, 28]\nc = [22, 25, 24, 21, 26, 23]\nres = stats.f_oneway(a, b, c)\nprint(f'F = {res.statistic:.2f}, p = {res.pvalue:.6f}')\ny = np.array(a + b + c)\nprint('means:', [round(float(np.mean(x)), 1) for x in (a, b, c)])\nprint(pairwise_tukeyhsd(y, ['A'] * 6 + ['B'] * 6 + ['C'] * 6).summary())\nssb = sum(len(x) * (np.mean(x) - y.mean()) ** 2 for x in (a, b, c))\nprint(f'eta squared = {ssb / ((y - y.mean()) ** 2).sum():.3f}')","answer":"Means are A = 21.5, B = 28.5 and C = 23.5 kg. ANOVA gives F = 22.29, p = 0.000032, so the fertilisers do not all give the same yield, and eta squared = 0.748 (about 75% of the variation goes with fertiliser). Tukey: B beats A by 7.0 (p < 0.001) and beats C by 5.0 (p = 0.0009), but A and C do not differ (p = 0.187). So B is the best of these.","out":"F = 22.29, p = 0.000032\nmeans: [21.5, 28.5, 23.5]\nMultiple Comparison of Means - Tukey HSD, FWER=0.05 \n====================================================\ngroup1 group2 meandiff p-adj   lower   upper  reject\n----------------------------------------------------\n     A      B      7.0    0.0  4.1944  9.8056   True\n     A      C      2.0 0.1871 -0.8056  4.8056  False\n     B      C     -5.0 0.0009 -7.8056 -2.1944   True\n----------------------------------------------------\neta squared = 0.748","figs":[]}}});
